(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-12-16"] = {"chem-12-16-m01":{"idea":"Green chemistry tries to stop pollution before it starts.","steps":["It is about designing products and processes.","The aim is to reduce or remove hazardous substances.","This covers both the use of such substances and their production.","So the correct choice talks about reducing hazards."],"others":"It is used in industry as well as laboratories. The colour green has nothing to do with it. Making as much waste as possible is the opposite of its aim.","trap":"Green here means environment-friendly, not the colour.","answer":"reduce or remove the use and the production of hazardous substances"},"chem-12-16-m02":{"idea":"The twelve principles of green chemistry have a named originator.","steps":["Paul Anastas, with John Warner, listed twelve principles in 1998.","He is called the father of green chemistry.","Taniguchi is linked to the word nanotechnology.","Pauling and Feynman are linked to other ideas."],"others":"Taniguchi first used the word nanotechnology. Pauling is not called the father of nanotechnology. Feynman gave a famous talk on small machines.","trap":"Anastas = green chemistry; Taniguchi = nanotechnology.","answer":"Paul Anastas, who is called the father of green chemistry"},"chem-12-16-m03":{"idea":"A principle of green chemistry never asks for more waste.","steps":["Green chemistry aims at the least waste.","Safer solvents, renewable raw materials and waste prevention are principles.","Making many by-products is against the aim.","So making as many by-products as possible is NOT a principle."],"others":"Safer solvents, renewable raw materials and preventing waste are all among the twelve principles.","trap":"Read the word NOT in the question.","answer":"producing as many by-products as possible, to sell them"},"chem-12-16-m04":{"idea":"Sustainable development looks after today and tomorrow together.","steps":["It meets the needs of people now.","It does this without harming future generations.","It balances growth, the environment and people's needs.","Green chemistry supports it by using fewer resources and making less waste."],"others":"Stopping all industry is not the aim. It is not only for rich countries. Using all resources quickly harms the future.","trap":"Key words: future generations.","answer":"without harming the ability of future generations to meet their own needs"},"chem-12-16-m05":{"idea":"Atom economy tells how much of the reactant mass ends up in the useful product.","steps":["Atom economy = (formula mass of desired product / sum of formula masses of all reactants) × 100.","It uses the balanced equation only.","A higher value means less waste."],"others":"Waste over product is not atom economy. Reactants over product would give more than 100 percent. Obtained over expected mass is the percentage yield.","trap":"Atom economy is a calculation on paper, not an experiment.","answer":"(formula mass of the desired product/sum of the formula masses of all the reactants) × 100"},"chem-12-16-m06":{"idea":"100 percent atom economy means every reactant atom ends up in the product.","steps":["The product contains all atoms of the reactants.","So there are no by-products.","An addition reaction such as C₂H₄ + H₂ → C₂H₆ is an example.","The experimental yield can still be below 100 percent."],"others":"Atom economy does not guarantee a 100 percent yield. It says nothing about speed, catalysts or heat. Being exothermic is a different property.","trap":"Atom economy is not the same as yield.","answer":"all the atoms of the reactants appear in the desired product, and there are no by-products"},"chem-12-16-m07":{"idea":"Atom economy comes from the equation, while yield comes from the experiment.","steps":["Atom economy uses formula masses from the balanced equation.","Percentage yield uses the mass actually obtained.","So atom economy does not depend on the experiment.","A reaction can have 100 percent yield and still a poor atom economy."],"others":"They are not the same, even at the same temperature. Atom economy is not always smaller than yield. Depending on the amount obtained describes yield.","trap":"Equation = atom economy; experiment = yield.","answer":"depends only on the balanced equation, and not on the experiment"},"chem-12-16-m08":{"idea":"Green chemistry prevents pollution at the source.","steps":["It cuts waste and hazards during the making of chemicals.","Prevention is better than cleaning up later.","It also lowers the cost of waste treatment."],"others":"Cleaning up later, moving waste to another country and burning it in the open all leave the pollution problem in place.","trap":"Prevention, not cure.","answer":"preventing it at its source, and not by cleaning it up later"},"chem-12-16-m09":{"idea":"A catalyst helps a reaction run with less energy and is not used up.","steps":["A catalyst lowers the activation energy.","So the reaction goes faster, with less energy.","The catalyst is not used up, and a small amount is enough.","This makes less waste than a reagent that is used up."],"others":"A catalyst is not used up. It does not slow the reaction. It does not make hazardous products.","trap":"Catalyst: faster, less energy, reusable.","answer":"makes a reaction faster at lower energy, and is not used up"},"chem-12-16-m10":{"idea":"The prefix nano means one billionth.","steps":["Nano = 10⁻⁹.","So 1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m.","For comparison, a DNA helix is about 2 nm wide."],"others":"10⁻¹² m is a picometre, 10⁻⁶ m is a micrometre and 10⁻³ m is a millimetre.","trap":"Nano 10⁻⁹, micro 10⁻⁶, milli 10⁻³.","answer":"10⁻⁹ m"},"chem-12-16-m11":{"idea":"Nanomaterials are defined by size: at least one dimension in a fixed range.","steps":["The nanoscale runs from about 1 nm to 100 nm.","At least one dimension of a nanomaterial lies in it.","Properties in this range can differ from the bulk."],"others":"1 μm to 100 μm and 100 nm to 1000 nm are too large. 0.01 nm to 0.1 nm is smaller than an atom.","trap":"Remember 1 to 100 nm.","answer":"1 nm to 100 nm"},"chem-12-16-m12":{"idea":"Count the dimensions that are not at the nanoscale.","steps":["A nanoparticle is nano in all three sizes, so it is 0D.","A nanowire is nano in two sizes and long in one, so it is 1D.","A thin film is nano in one size and large in two, so it is 2D.","The order is 0D, 1D, 2D."],"others":"The other choices put the numbers in a wrong order or make all three one-dimensional.","trap":"0D is a point-like particle.","answer":"zero-dimensional, one-dimensional and two-dimensional"},"chem-12-16-m13":{"idea":"At the nanoscale, the properties of a material depend on its size.","steps":["Colour comes from how the electrons respond to light.","This response depends on the size and shape of the particle.","Gold nanoparticles of different sizes give red or purple.","The element is still gold."],"others":"No dye is used. The atoms do not change, and gold does not become another element.","trap":"Same element, different size, different property.","answer":"the properties of a material depend on the size when the particles are very small"},"chem-12-16-m14":{"idea":"Cutting a solid into smaller pieces increases its total surface area.","steps":["For small cubes of side a that make up volume V, total area = 6V/a.","A smaller a gives a larger area.","A side 1000 times smaller makes the area 1000 times larger.","More surface means faster reaction and better catalysis."],"others":"The area does not decrease, stay the same or become zero. Nanoparticles are reactive because of the large area.","trap":"Smaller pieces = more area for the same volume.","answer":"increases, which is why nanoparticles are very reactive"},"chem-12-16-m15":{"idea":"Many atoms of a nanoparticle are at the surface, and these are loosely held.","steps":["A large fraction of atoms is at the surface.","Surface atoms are held less tightly.","So the melting point is lower.","The large surface also makes the nanoparticle more reactive."],"others":"Nanoparticles do have a melting point and are reactive. The same melting point and reactivity would hide the size effect. A higher melting point goes against the surface effect.","trap":"Small size: lower melting point, higher reactivity.","answer":"a lower melting point, and a higher reactivity"},"chem-12-16-m16":{"idea":"Building up atom by atom is called the bottom-up approach.","steps":["Start with atoms or molecules.","Build them into a nanomaterial.","This is the bottom-up approach.","Growing nanoparticles from a colloidal solution is an example."],"others":"Top-down is the opposite: a bulk piece is cut smaller. Inside-out and sideways are not standard names.","trap":"Bottom-up: from small to large.","answer":"the bottom-up approach"},"chem-12-16-m17":{"idea":"Grinding a bulk solid to fine pieces starts from large and goes to small.","steps":["Start with a large piece.","Break or cut it down to nano size.","This is the top-down approach.","Ball milling and lithography are other examples."],"others":"Bottom-up builds from atoms. The sol-gel process is a wet chemical method. Grinding is not wet chemical synthesis.","trap":"Top-down: from large to small.","answer":"the top-down approach"},"chem-12-16-m18":{"idea":"In the sol-gel process a liquid sol turns into a connected gel.","steps":["A sol is a colloidal solution of tiny solid particles.","It changes into a gel, a network of particles in the liquid.","The gel is dried and heated.","This gives the nanomaterial as a powder, film or fibre."],"others":"Burning removes the particles, which is not the aim. Turning to a gas and condensing is a different method. Evaporating to a pure liquid is not possible with solid particles.","trap":"Sol → gel → dry → nanomaterial.","answer":"changed into a gel, a connected network of the particles in the liquid"},"chem-12-16-m19":{"idea":"The word nanotechnology has a known first user and year.","steps":["Norio Taniguchi used the word in 1974.","He used it for processing materials with nanometre precision.","Nanomaterials were used long before, as in old red glass."],"others":"Feynman gave a talk in 1960 but did not use the word. Anastas is linked to 1998 and green chemistry. Pauling is linked to 1954.","trap":"Taniguchi 1974.","answer":"Norio Taniguchi, in 1974"},"chem-12-16-m20":{"idea":"Visible light is too coarse to see nanoscale details, but electrons are not.","steps":["Visible light has wavelength 400 to 700 nm.","This is larger than nanoscale objects.","Electrons have a very short wavelength.","So electron microscopes (SEM and TEM) can see nanomaterials."],"others":"A telescope looks at far objects. A magnifying glass and a simple optical microscope use visible light, which cannot resolve nanoscale structure.","trap":"Use electrons for the nanoscale.","answer":"the scanning electron microscope (SEM), or the transmission electron microscope (TEM)"},"chem-12-16-m21":{"idea":"The lotus leaf has tiny structures that repel water and dirt.","steps":["Lotus leaves have tiny bumps at the nanoscale.","Water drops stay round on these bumps.","The drops roll off and take dust with them.","This is the lotus effect, copied in self-cleaning paints and glass."],"others":"The banana leaf is not the model. Large holes that absorb water would not clean the surface. A sticky spider web does the opposite.","trap":"Lotus effect: water repelled, dirt removed.","answer":"the lotus leaf, whose tiny structures repel water and dirt"},"chem-12-16-m22":{"idea":"Silver nanoparticles kill bacteria.","steps":["Silver ions come out of the nanoparticles.","They damage the cells of bacteria.","So they help to purify drinking water.","They are also used in bandages and fabrics."],"others":"They do not colour the water or make it sweet. They cannot remove the salt of sea water completely.","trap":"Silver = antibacterial.","answer":"kill bacteria, and so help to purify drinking water"},"chem-12-16-m23":{"idea":"Very small particles can go where bigger ones cannot.","steps":["Nanoparticles can enter the lungs, the blood and even cells.","Breathing them in may damage the lungs.","The effect depends on size, shape and chemical nature."],"others":"Nanoparticles can enter the body. They do not make all materials weaker. They are small, not too big to be harmful.","trap":"Small size is both a use and a risk.","answer":"nanoparticles that are breathed in may damage the lungs"},"chem-12-16-n01":{"idea":"Atom economy = mass of desired product / total mass of reactants × 100.","steps":["Reactants = 60 + 46 = 106.","Desired product = 88.","Atom economy = 88 / 106 × 100.","= 83.0 percent."],"trap":"Check: the by-product is 18, and 1 − 18/106 = 0.830.","answer":"83.0 %"},"chem-12-16-n02":{"idea":"Atom economy compares the desired product with all the reactants.","steps":["Reactants = 16 + 71 = 87.","Desired product CH₃Cl = 50.5.","Atom economy = 50.5 / 87 × 100.","= 58.0 percent."],"trap":"Check: 1 − 36.5/87 = 0.580, since HCl is the waste.","answer":"58.0 %"},"chem-12-16-n03":{"idea":"For a sphere, the area to volume ratio is 3 / r.","steps":["Area = 4πr², volume = (4/3)πr³.","Ratio = 4πr² / ((4/3)πr³) = 3 / r.","r = 5.0 nm.","Ratio = 3 / 5.0 = 0.60 nm⁻¹."],"trap":"Check: a bigger sphere has a smaller ratio, so small particles have more surface per volume.","answer":"0.60 nm⁻¹"},"chem-12-16-n04":{"idea":"Find the volume, then the mass, then the moles, then the atoms.","steps":["Radius = 2.5 nm. Volume = (4/3)π(2.5)³ = 65.4 nm³.","1 nm³ = 10⁻²¹ cm³, so volume = 6.54 × 10⁻²⁰ cm³.","Mass = 19.3 × 6.54 × 10⁻²⁰ = 1.26 × 10⁻¹⁸ g.","Moles = 1.26 × 10⁻¹⁸ / 197 = 6.41 × 10⁻²¹ mol.","Atoms = 6.41 × 10⁻²¹ × 6.022 × 10²³ ≈ 3861."],"trap":"Check: gold is face-centred cubic with 4 atoms per cell of edge 0.408 nm. That gives about 3855 atoms.","answer":"3861"}};
